WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.451
Keep keys out of your code.

2
00:00:01.731 --> 00:00:09.987
An API key is a bearer credential: anyone who has it can spend your money and read whatever your organization’s data settings allow.

3
00:00:10.267 --> 00:00:11.632
Treat it like a password.

4
00:00:11.912 --> 00:00:22.557
The SDK reads ANTHROPIC_API_KEY from the environment when you construct the client with no arguments, so the key never needs to appear in source code.

5
00:00:22.837 --> 00:00:25.035
For these labs, put it in labs/.

6
00:00:25.315 --> 00:00:28.749
env, which is gitignored, and let the shared test setup load it.

7
00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:36.069
In deployed services, use your platform’s secret manager and inject the key as an environment variable at runtime.

8
00:00:36.349 --> 00:00:38.696
Three rules cover most real incidents.

9
00:00:38.976 --> 00:00:53.547
Never commit a key, including in notebooks, test fixtures, or a “temporary” script; if one leaks, revoke it in the Console and issue a new one, because deleting the commit does not remove it from history or from anyone who cloned it.

10
00:00:53.827 --> 00:00:58.819
Never put a key in a prompt, a log line, or an error message you send to a user.

11
00:00:59.099 --> 00:01:09.488
And use separate keys, ideally in separate workspaces with their own spend limits, for development and production, so a runaway test loop cannot drain your production budget.

12
00:01:09.768 --> 00:01:15.272
The Console lets you set a monthly spend limit below your tier’s cap for exactly this reason.
